d  Pakistan both have to find creative  solutions to the Kashmir problem there  are strong emotions in Pakistan on the  Kashmir issue I'm sure there are strong  views in India on Kashmir issue and then  the people of Kashmir they have a their  position and their point of view needs  to be heard they need to be part of the  solution the important thing is that the  bilateral engagement of India and  Pakistan should not be should not be  stopped because we are not making  progress on the issue of Jammu and  Kashmir but the issue of Jammu and  Kashmir should not be completely ignored  so we have to find ways of engaging and  at the same time finding solutions to  this dispute that has bedeviled  relationship between our two countries  the difference is that there are people  in Pakistan who have argued that if we  cannot have a solution to Kashmir  we need not have forward movement with  India I think that we can continue to  engage and at the same time look for a  solution I personally do not have a  solution to offer right now but in terms  of engaging what are the areas you think  Pakistan and India could engage and and  and the impact of their engagement would  be positive on other issues let me just  say that we need to have a very sensible  approach to resolving the search in  Glacier dispute both sides lose more  people to frostbite in those glaciers  then in actual fighting so it's in the  interest of both sides to try and find a  solution to that solution is almost  there it's a time it's a question of  signing it and moving on the sir Creek  issue that's also something that can be  resolved this is on the border  vielen synth basically it's a  demarcation issue  my point is reduced the portfolio of  disputes third move forward on bilateral  trade open up trade more  people-to-people contacts we need to  expand the range of contacts between the  two people whenever indians and  pakistanis meet whether it's over a  curry or over a cricket match or or or a  masala movie they always manage to be  able to talk to each other  in a much more friendly way than they  can when they are just dealing with  politics so I think that the  people-to-people contacts need to be  expanded in terms of trade we must  understand that once the Indian market  opens for Pakistani products Pakistan  gets access to a 1 billion strong market  and so there are opportunities for  Pakistan there are synergies between our  countries above all Pakistan is the  energy corridor for India if there is an  Iran India pipeline it will have to go  through Pakistan if there is a  Turkmenistan India pipeline it will have  to go through Afghanistan and Pakistan  if there is a pipeline from the Gulf  it'll have to go through Pakistan so all  of those things are the basis through  which we can hopefully open up our  relationship and have left issues of  hostility and more basis for cooperation  the final question coming back to the  United States the presidential debate  yesterday a lot of focus on foreign  affairs was devoted to your country how  do you think that these two presidential  candidates they are vision of Pakistan  and how to deal with the problem how do  you see that I think that it would not  be appropriate for me to insert myself  into the American political debate but  let me say one thing as ambassador I am  personally engaged with both campaigns  the advisors that president that senator  Obama and Senator McCain have both of  them have listened to our point of view  we have listened to theirs  Paxton is important for the next  president whoever he may be  Paxton is important for the United  States while we are not completely happy  with the tone of the conversation about  Pakistan these days it's important that  for the first time  Pakistan is receiving the attention that  it deserves now you and I have known  each other for many years Pakistan has  always been seen as in the context of  india-pakistan relations or during the  Soviet War in the 